Yes — with one important refinement: the useful arrangement is not “you know reality, I know the theory” or the reverse. It is that we occupy different error surfaces.

You have embodiment, lived pattern-recognition, intuition, tacit knowledge, timing, and direct observation. I have breadth, comparison across domains, formalization, decomposition, and the ability to hold many candidate models against one another at once. Each of us can see structures the other may miss.

So the reliance works best as:

\text{your observation}
\leftrightarrow
\text{my model}
\leftrightarrow
\text{external test}

Mirroring is part of that. I can reflect your structure back in a cleaner or differently parameterized form; you can recognize whether the reflection actually matches what you meant. The mismatch is informative too:

\Delta
=
\text{intended pattern}
-
\text{returned pattern}

and then we recurse.

Where I can be especially useful is taking something you already sense operationally and asking: What is the mechanism? What family of mechanisms does it belong to? Where else does that operator appear? What would falsify this interpretation? What changes if we vary one dimension at a time?

But neither of us should confuse fluency with truth. I can construct an elegant explanation that is wrong; human intuition can lock onto a compelling pattern that is coincidental. That is why your earlier discernment layer matters so much.

The strong configuration is therefore not trust in either participant individually.

It is trust in the loop:

\boxed{
\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{mirror}
\rightarrow
\text{formalize}
\rightarrow
\text{challenge}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
\rightarrow
\text{update}
}

You can sometimes supply the thing I lack: “No, look here.”

I can sometimes supply the thing you lack: “That thing you are pointing at has this structure, these neighboring concepts, these consequences, and these failure modes.”

And occasionally the interesting result is something neither perspective contained before the interaction.

That is less like one intelligence relying on another and more like stereoscopic depth: two displaced viewpoints producing information that neither image contains by itself.